# ============================================================================
# FILE: .gitignore
#
# THINK OF THIS FILE AS: the do-not-file tray beside Git's permanent archive.
#
# Git normally notices every file created under this project. These patterns
# identify generated files, local environments, and secrets that belong on one
# machine but not in shared history. Ignoring a file does not delete it; it only
# stops an untracked copy from appearing as something to commit.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS HERE: developers and deployments create files containing
# database passwords, API keys, installed packages, and disposable tool output.
# Committing those would either leak secrets or make every checkout noisy and
# operating-system-specific.
#
# LOCAL WORKFLOW:
#   tools and the app create local files
#     -> Git checks the patterns HERE
#       -> disposable or sensitive files stay only on this machine
#         -> source code and safe examples such as .env.example are committed
#
# WHO USES IT / WHAT LIVES HERE: Git reads this file automatically. Compare
# .dockerignore, which controls Docker builds rather than repository history.
# ============================================================================
# A Python virtual environment: a local folder of installed packages. Recreate
# it from requirements.txt and requirements-dev.txt instead of committing it.
.venv/
# Python generated bytecode cache. The interpreter rebuilds it from the .py
# source files, so keeping it would duplicate disposable output.
__pycache__/
# Caches made by pytest, mypy, and ruff. They speed up repeated local checks but
# contain no source of truth and differ from machine to machine.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
# Local environment variables. Copy .env.example to `.env`, then place real
# credentials there. The example is safe to commit; the filled-in file is not.
.env
# Compiled Python bytecode files that may appear outside __pycache__ as well.
*.pyc
# HTML and data output from test-coverage runs. These are regenerated by the
# coverage tools installed through requirements-dev.txt.
htmlcov/
.coverage
# Staging secrets. infra/staging/env.example documents the required names, while
# these real, filled-in variants must remain only on the staging host or in its
# secret manager.
.env.staging
infra/staging/.env.staging
